USAGE CONDITIONS
Public interceptorPlugin/mockerPlugin on a reachable Vite development-server WebSocket can accept manual mock/redirect registration.
Manual URLs/export names were emitted into module source without proper escaping; Windows backslash paths could bypass the file allowlist.
Upstream states standard Node vitest runs do not register these public WS handlers; production CLI/App omit Vitest.
This limits current exposure but does not make development-dependency advisories harmless. No audit gate is disabled or lowered.

MINIMAL CHANGE
vitest exact 4.1.11 -> 4.1.12 and seven matching @vitest packages to 4.1.12.
No overrides or production dependency changes. All unrelated lock entries and original nested picomatch/fdir resolutions preserved.
Initial npm install deduplicated unrelated nested nodes; final lock restored those unchanged nodes and was verified by clean npm ci.
No major migration, Node-policy change, release-tag/asset edit or npm/image publication.

TARGETED REGRESSION (tests/unit/mocker-security.test.ts)
5 cases against installed public APIs, no network listener and no generated payload evaluation:
- adversarial URL stays a single string argument;
- quoted/newline export names remain valid module syntax/data;
- inside-root redirect positive control;
- forward-slash traversal blocked;
- Windows backslash traversal blocked.
4.1.11 negative control: EXIT1, 3 failed / 2 passed. Only synthetic outside-root canary was read in the local Windows reproduction.
4.1.12: 5 passed. New test standalone tsc noEmit: EXIT0.
Owned negative-control copy removed from primary checkout after hash verification; temporary canary directories cleaned in finally with path guard.

